\f0\fs36 \cf0 "Interrupts" are out-of-band / spontaneous changes to program control flow.  Like "On Error Goto" in applesoft and other BASICS.  When an interrupt happens, the current statement finishes executing, then a GOSUB happens to a previously registered line number.  Once the interrupt has been serviced, then you RETURN to resume the program where it left off.\
\
There's four different kinds of interrupts. Three are device related\
* SRQ - 
\f1\b System Request
\f0\b0 . Some piece of hardware wants attention, so it raises its virtual hand saying "hey!  I need to tell you something"\
* EOI - 
\f1\b End Or Identify
\f0\b0  (sometimes referred to End Or Identity).  Basically, some device has ended its communication, whether it has delivered the last byte of a data payload, or delivered the last byte of a an identify string.\
* EOF - 
\f1\b End of File
\f0\b0 . When doing an INPUT from tape, you've consumed the last record, and then you try to consume a record that doesn't exist\
* SIZE - The only software interrupt, happens when you have numeric overflow (exceeding 1 += E304).  By default, overflowing like this will terminate the program with an error.  If you know how to handle that case (or you're processing data, that with user error can overflow some calculation), then you can branch to a "hey!  you messed up!" handler and perhaps try to figure out what's wrong, or start the process over from the beginning an give the user a second chance to make a good impression.\
\
Register an interrupt handler with ON interrupt-name THEN line-number.  Say you wanted to have a handler for each of the interrupts.\
\
100 ON SRQ THEN 1000\
110 ON EOI THEN 1100\
120 ON EOF THEN 1200\
130 ON SIZE THEN 1300\
...\
1000 REM Handle System Request\
1099 RETURN\
\
1100 REM Handle EOI\
1199 RETURN\
\
etc.\
\
So, what do you do when a handler gets called?  For EOF, you know to stop gathering input, and perhaps move on to more intensive processing.  For a SIZE, you don't really know 
\f2\i what
\f0\i0  caused the problem, so you'll need to adapt somehow (most likely by reevaluating whatever data you have, filtering it, and restarting your work). \
\
For EOI, I don't know what you're supposed to do.\
\
For SRQ, there's a status byte that could be interesting. To read it, you'll want to POLL interesting devices to see which one needs attention.  POLL takes a couple of "out parameter" variables (that get filled in my the call before it returns to you), as well as a list of device addresses that you want to query.\
For example:\
100 POLL D, R; 13; 22; 55; 5\
\
POLL will check each device address in the order they're given. "Hey device 13, you need anything? No?  ok, device 22, you need anything?  You do? Cool!".  When POLL returns, D (or whatever legal variable name you use) will be filled in with the 
\f2\i position in the list of device addresses
\f0\i0  (not the actual address) of the device that triggered the SRQ.  The other variable is filled with the SRQ payload.  \
\
So, riffing on the prior example\
100 ON SRQ THEN 1000\
...\
1000 REM Handle System Request\
1010 POLL A9,D9;13;22;55;5\
1020 IF A9 = 13 THEN GOTO 1500  
\f2\i (or whatever the handler for device 13 is, which knows to look in D9 for the value)
\f0\i0 \
\
Don't forget that BASIC is one big happy namespace, and if someone in a far-flung part of the program uses the same variable name that your handler does, you could have a difficult to track down bug.  This is why the example uses A9/D9 (address/data).  Presumably folks who are using multiple D-named variables will be using D0, D1, etc, the variable names that occur numerically earlier.\

\f2\i come to think of it, that could be a way of namespacing things - particular subroutines / modules / etc could stake claim to their own variable digit.   
